# The fusions whose parts are not disjoint, with the layer they both reach. One row per (fusion,
# layer reached through two of its own parts), carrying which two parts got there.
#
# Where the partition claim is false
#   A fusion's parts claim to partition the composed layer, and this is where that claim is false.
#   Not a duplicate: a layer somebody referred to at one level while a sibling part already
#   carried it, so the partition has an overlap nobody wrapped. `asrt:Elimination` is the filer
#   admitting an overlap and sizing it. This is an overlap that is structural, visible in the
#   incidence, and unadmitted.
#
# The subject is the fusion, not the root above it
#   Every layer above the fusion also reaches the doubled layer twice, so a rule keyed on the root
#   reports one defect once per ancestor and names a composer who did nothing wrong. The composer
#   at fault is the one holding both parts, which is exactly the key here.
#
# The reach is reflexive on purpose
#   Which is why this composes `composition/reachable.sqlc`, F*, rather than
#   `composition/descent.sqlc`, F+. Naming a layer directly while a sibling composes it is the
#   canonical case, so a part must count
#   as reaching itself or the commonest form of this escapes. `merge-holding-composition/compute`
#   naming `merge-us-member/compute` beside `merge-group-composition/compute-us` is that case, and
#   it is in this corpus: the holding reaches one member's layer directly and reaches it again
#   through the group that composes it. A population written as the parts a fusion does not name
#   cannot see it, because the reflexive reach is a part the fusion does name.
#
# A key refuses it at one level, nothing at two
#   At one level the primary key on `pm.part` already refuses this, and nothing can at two, because
#   the second path runs through a document the first does not contain. That is the whole reason
#   this is a query.
#
# Not conditioned on a filed elimination
#   The key governs the redeclaration and `e` governs the number, and folding an answer about a
#   magnitude into a structural population is how a rule stops being able to see the structure.
#
# The pair comparison
#   It keeps the result a set of unordered pairs and drops the reflexive one, so a part is never
#   reported against itself.
#
# Probe
#   Nest a doubled layer three levels deep: keyed on the fusion this returns one row, where a key
#   on the root would return one per ancestor, against filings that did nothing wrong.
-- composition/parts.sqlc self-joined on the fusion, against the reflexive closure of
-- composition/descent.sqlc, for the layer two sibling parts both reach.
SELECT DISTINCT
       a.composition    AS filing,
       a.composed_layer AS layer,
       r1.filing        AS doubled_filing,
       r1.layer         AS doubled_layer,
       a.part_filing    AS via_filing,
       a.part_layer     AS via_layer,
       b.part_filing    AS also_via_filing,
       b.part_layer     AS also_via_layer
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) a
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) b ON  b.composition    = a.composition
    AND b.composed_layer = a.composed_layer
    AND (a.part_filing, a.part_layer) < (b.part_filing, b.part_layer)
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/reachable.sqlc)
) r1 ON r1.root_filing = a.part_filing AND r1.root_layer = a.part_layer
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/reachable.sqlc)
) r2 ON  r2.root_filing = b.part_filing AND r2.root_layer = b.part_layer
     AND r2.filing = r1.filing AND r2.layer = r1.layer
